.
.Sh REPL
The REPL reads a line at a time and evaluates each form once
it is complete.
The prompt shows the line number and any open delimiters.
Documentation for a binding is shown with
.Ql "\*(lpdoc symbol\*(rp" ,
and
.Ql "\*(lpdoc\*(rp"
lists the bindings in scope.
.Pp
Prompts are written to standard error,
or to standard output with
.Fl s .
.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Xo
.Ev WATTLE_PATH
.Xc
A list of root directories,
separated by
.Ql "\&:" ,
or by
.Ql "\&;"
on Windows.
System modules are loaded from
.Ql "lib/wattle"
under each.
The last directory becomes
.Ql "*syspath*"
and is searched first.
If unset,
.Ql "*syspath*"
is the value the build was made with:
a path,
none,
or nothing yet.
A build with none loads no system modules.
A build with nothing yet,
which is the default,
raises an error that names
.Ev WATTLE_PATH
at the first system module lookup or
.Ic pkg
command.
.It Xo
.Ev WATTLE_HISTFILE
.Xc
A file to keep the REPL’s history in.
Each form submitted at the REPL on a terminal is appended to
it,
and the forms in it are recalled with the Up key in a later
session.
If unset,
history lasts only until the REPL exits.
.It Xo
.Ev NO_COLOR
.Xc
If set to a non-empty value,
disable colour.
Colour is otherwise used when both standard output and standard
error are terminals.
.El
.Pp
An environment variable set to the empty string is treated as
unset.
.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits
.Ql "0"
on success and
.Ql "1"
if a script,
a subcommand or an option raises an error that is not handled.
An error in the REPL is printed and does not end the session.
A program can choose its own status with
.Ql "\*(lpos/exit status\*(rp" .
.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Start the REPL:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ wattle
.Ed
.Pp
Run a script with two arguments:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ wattle hello\&.wattle Alice Bob
.Ed
.Pp
Evaluate an expression and print its result:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ wattle -e \(aq\*(lpprint \*(lp+ 1 2 3\*(rp\*(rp\(aq
.Ed
.Pp
Compile a program into an image and run the image:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ wattle build img hello\&.wattle hello\&.jimage
$ wattle run -i hello\&.jimage Alice
.Ed
.Pp
Build the executables that
.Ql "info\&.edn"
declares,
optimised for size:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ wattle -s /usr/local build exe --release small
.Ed
.Pp
Check a script for compile errors without running it,
stopping at the first:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ wattle check --bail hello\&.wattle
.Ed
.Pp
Run the tests in
.Ql "\&./test" ,
skipping one file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ wattle test --no-file slow\&.wattle
.Ed
.Pp
Install a package and list the installed packages:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ wattle pkg install \&./my-pkg
$ wattle pkg list
.Ed
.
.Sh SEE ALSO
Wattle does not yet have a written manual.
The core library documents itself:
.Ql "\*(lpdoc symbol\*(rp"
prints a binding’s documentation in the REPL and
.Ql "\*(lpdoc\*(rp"
lists the bindings in scope.
Example programs are distributed with the source at
.Lk https://github.com/pyrmont/wattle .
.
.Sh HISTORY
Wattle began as a fork of Janet,
the language created by Calvin Rose.
It has its own version numbering:
the binding
.Ql "wattle/version"
reports that version.
